{"step_index":1351,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T21:59:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T05:59:35+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T05:59:41+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T05:59:35+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T05:59:41+08:00\nThe search for \"master detail web UI back button vs top nav tab navigation URL history replaceState vanilla JS\" returned the following summary:\nWhen designing a **Master-Detail web UI** with tab navigation using vanilla JavaScript, managing the browser's history correctly is vital[1]. A common UX bug is \"back-button hijacking\"—where clicking through tabs or detail views forces the user to press the back button dozens of times just to leave the page[2].\n\nThe core architectural choice comes down to knowing when to use **`history.pushState()`** versus **`history.replaceState()`**[3].\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Core Rules: `pushState` vs `replaceState`\n\n*   **`pushState()` (Create a new history entry):** Use this when the user navigates to a **genuinely new context or drill-down** (e.g., clicking a Master item to open the Detail view). The user expects the browser’s Back button to return them to the previous master state[3].\n*   **`replaceState()` (Modify the current history entry):** Use this when switching **tabs, filters, or sub-views *within* the same context** (e.g., switching from the \"Overview\" tab to the \"Settings\" tab inside a Detail view)[3]. This keeps the URL updated for bookmarking/sharing without cluttering the browser's history stack with redundant steps[4][3][1].\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommended Navigation Flow Pattern\n\n1.  **List to Detail (Master Click):** Use **`pushState`**. \n    *   *Why:* Going from a list of 20 items to inspecting Item A is a structural navigation step. Back should close/leave the detail view.\n2.  **Tab Switching inside Detail:** Use **`replaceState`**.\n    *   *Why:* If a user clicks \"Overview\" $\\rightarrow$ \"Activity\" $\\rightarrow$ \"Settings\" inside Item A's detail page, clicking \"Back\" should take them *back to the Master list*, not cycle backward through Item A's tabs[3].\n\n---\n\n### 3. Vanilla JavaScript Implementation Blueprint\n\nHere is how you wire up a master-detail interface using vanilla JavaScript, URL manipulation, and the `popstate` event[4][1]:\n\n#### HTML Structure Skeleton\n```html\n<!-- Master View -->\n<div id=\"master-view\">\n  <ul>\n    <li><a href=\"#\" class=\"item-link\" data-id=\"1\">Item 1</a></li>\n    <li><a href=\"#\" class=\"item-link\" data-id=\"2\">Item 2</a></li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<!-- Detail View (Hidden by default) -->\n<div id=\"detail-view\" style=\"display: none;\">\n  <button id=\"back-to-master\">← Back to List</button>\n  \n  <!-- Top Nav Tabs -->\n  <nav class=\"tabs\">\n    <button class=\"tab-btn\" data-tab=\"overview\">Overview</button>\n    <button class=\"tab-btn\" data-tab=\"activity\">Activity</button>\n  </nav>\n\n  <div id=\"tab-content\"></div>\n</div>\n```\n\n#### Vanilla JS Router & State Manager\n```javascript\nconst masterView = document.getElementById('master-view');\nconst detailView = document.getElementById('detail-view');\nconst tabContent = document.getElementById('tab-content');\n\n// 1. Handle Master Item Click (PUSH state)\ndocument.querySelectorAll('.item-link').forEach(link => {\n  link.addEventListener('click', (e) => {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    const itemId = e.target.getAttribute('data-id');\n    \n    // Push a new history entry because we are entering a detail context\n    const url = `/item/${itemId}?tab=overview`;\n    history.pushState({ itemId, tab: 'overview' }, '', url);\n    \n    renderDetail(itemId, 'overview');\n  });\n});\n\n// 2. Handle Tab Navigation Inside Detail (REPLACE state)\ndocument.querySelectorAll('.tab-btn').forEach(btn => {\n  btn.addEventListener('click', (e) => {\n    const tabName = e.target.getAttribute('data-tab');\n    const urlParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);\n    const itemId = urlParams.get('item') || getCurrentItemId(); // or extract from path\n\n    // Replace current state so tab changes don't spam the back-button stack\n    const url = `/item/${itemId}?tab=${tabName}`;\n    history.replaceState({ itemId, tab: tabName }, '', url);\n    \n    renderTabContent(tabName);\n  });\n});\n\n// 3. Handle Browser Back/Forward Buttons\nwindow.addEventListener('popstate', (event) => {\n  const state = event.state;\n\n  if (!state || !state.itemId) {\n    // User went back to the root Master view\n    masterView.style.display = 'block';\n    detailView.style.display = 'none';\n  } else {\n    // User navigated through history states inside details\n    masterView.style.display = 'none';\n    detailView.style.display = 'block';\n    renderDetail(state.itemId, state.tab);\n  }\n});\n\n// 4. Helper rendering functions\nfunction renderDetail(itemId, tab) {\n  masterView.style.display = 'none';\n  detailView.style.display = 'block';\n  // Fetch or populate data for itemId...\n  renderTabContent(tab);\n}\n\nfunction renderTabContent(tabName) {\n  document.querySelectorAll('.tab-btn').forEach(b => b.classList.remove('active'));\n  document.querySelector(`[data-tab=\"${tabName}\"]`)?.classList.add('active');\n  tabContent.innerHTML = `<p>Loading content for ${tabName}...</p>`;\n}\n\n// Optional: Handle initial page load matching current URL\nwindow.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {\n  const pathParts = window.location.pathname.split('/');\n  const itemId = pathParts[2]; // assuming /item/:id structure\n  const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);\n  const tab = params.get('tab') || 'overview';\n\n  if (itemId) {\n    renderDetail(itemId, tab);\n  }\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist for Best UX\n\n| Action Performed by User | History Method to Use | Expected Back Button Behavior |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Master $\\rightarrow$ Detail** | `history.pushState()`[3] | Returns user back to the master list[3]. |\n| **Switching Tabs (Overview $\\leftrightarrow$ Settings)** | `history.replaceState()`[3] | Skips intermediate tabs and returns user directly to the master list[3]. |\n| **Applying Filters / Search Queries** | `history.replaceState()`[3] | Updates URL for link sharing, but pressing back exits the filtered view instead of undoing every keystroke/filter toggle[3][2]. |\n\nSources:\n[1] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFBb_NxOjv6PHXanyjbVDHhJW6WPC0EIk6Ut-fb7t1JY905Ju6u_MIMGzTwoJZI2PF5iC-NH9SQwwqkAUi3Rc3X8u01MPXvTPq82d_YbZ0muQlQD_lEvTyjDISOXg82hkqPf5DrFUIJh1GTracWupQrf6kDxapSXUHUg2leBCghPOX294EMzDElKFSRrv0Pv3FoyBu8CAkTZXKHM7kipb0Z3xbbRR2mfnLy9ch0KrjU5JSegXGTFIc=)\n[2] [reddit.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHbmZhvK9IK5bl4lFVKlAGVu9wwurNU_JmyVY56LQOO2Zo6swFmM_sS1WT73bq3PbxcVss5-cJ5CIsoeJu_fGEbp-SbpQbWZfzbfuJ4v9jJXWcIcOegdBvvdhYC8QTpoi7HJ7cC17oZ79Y2E839gW0FSQ28CAoQEFQiIX_Jh610JlBCZThJtvGQb7EKGsiIXkwTjQ==)\n[3] [frontend-routing.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG4pJJRKXrX2ZNONYV0ckDl0xkTyTeZYoBWLSySQpzfEJJaCCPmRPHZsp7dLe8eg-AiYCFOwtP4sQSvFu333kRcrTtHGb7cf8JUgOLSQbY0kjq5lBrxg1agazZvMjhkRsxD6tqPQjnjwRWMEPI7qiMHlhiUARfezNlTJMswgVMDmG1BnOlpZQF_Fa6L2e66eVTAJEwlsuNCxJRcR2s6JKv4NaIl0SJTmPc_NPY6sMxfzoTZ__rweNJfnQciTEY=)\n[4] [willschenk.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHhZ5GJTDQ6R6Lgkc0UB4v39plvTzWD17lRjyuygLCTw819LgLcoyyahW63M7NUnix7iaduyhkGnmwzy248Kn6yFuORwO3ZJLWNz8-o6YWigLTkB0kjdDUkO6aPKgnHzZ9wjGgVhvGXUZEPemg=)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
